**Breakdown:**

- `sqlmap` invokes the tool
- `'http://154.57.164.66:38061/case8.php'` is the target URL
- `-X POST` forces the request method to POST
- `-H '...'` flags replicate the headers the browser originally sent, including the session cookie
- `--data-raw 'id=1*&t0ken=LefKohTty90CKm7vIZctU37m0LjpjwF0gZg7j0n4'` sends the POST body, with `*` marking the injection point in `id` and the original token value included as a starting point
- `--batch` skips every interactive prompt by automatically choosing the default option
- `--dump` dumps the content of the specified table
- `-T flag8` specifies the `flag8` table
- `--csrf-token="t0ken"` names the token parameter, so SQLMap automatically parses a fresh token out of each response and uses it on the next request
- `--flush-session` discards any previously stored session data, forcing a fresh run

As a whole, supplying `--csrf-token` with the correct, non-standard parameter name let SQLMap keep the anti-CSRF token valid across its hundreds of test requests, where it would otherwise have been rejected after the very first one.

